# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
# Copyright (c) 2026 Brighton Sikarskie
#
# pins.env -- single source of truth for the ESP32-C6 wireless co-processor
# firmware: the pinned upstream versions, the RA8-host <-> C6 SPI pin map, the
# SW4 mux positions that map depends on, and the bench USB cabling that makes
# flashing work. Anything a person has to get physically right belongs here as
# DATA, not as prose in whichever script or README happened to need it.
#
# build.sh and flash.sh source this file (KEY=value shell fragment; no shebang,
# not a standalone script). Keep it pure KEY=value so it stays source-able.

# ---- Pinned upstream (esp-hosted-mcu SOUP; see docs/SOUP/esp-hosted.md) ----
ESP_HOSTED_MCU_URL=https://github.com/espressif/esp-hosted-mcu
ESP_HOSTED_MCU_COMMIT=949bb30612747a3bd9e402eda8d01fbfa1f8503e
ESP_HOSTED_MCU_SHORT=949bb30
ESP_HOSTED_MCU_FW_VERSION=2.12.11
ESP_IDF_VERSION=v5.5.4
ESP_TARGET=esp32c6

# ---- SPI transport pin map (ESP32-C6 GPIO numbers) ----
# Inclusive names first, with the Espressif legacy name in parentheses.
#   CS   (Chip Select)                = GPIO0
#   COPI (Controller Out / MOSI)      = GPIO1
#   CIPO (Controller In  / MISO)      = GPIO2
#   SCK  (clock / CLK)                = GPIO3
#   DATA_READY (slave -> host)        = GPIO4
#   HANDSHAKE  (slave -> host)        = GPIO6
#   RESET                             = disconnected (-1)
#
# NOTE: in sdkconfig.defaults the settable leaves are ESP_SPI_HSPI_GPIO_{CS,
# MOSI,MISO,CLK}; the ESP_SPI_GPIO_{CS,MOSI,MISO,CLK} symbols are DERIVED and
# ignored. DATA_READY and HANDSHAKE are directly settable (ESP_SPI_GPIO_*).
C6_PIN_CS=0
C6_PIN_COPI=1
C6_PIN_CIPO=2
C6_PIN_SCK=3
C6_PIN_DATA_READY=4
C6_PIN_HANDSHAKE=6
C6_PIN_RESET=-1

# ---- Dev-board preset: the setting that decides whether the pins above run ----
# esp-hosted-mcu ships Kconfig presets for Espressif's own dev boards, and
# selecting one OVERRIDES every SPI pin leaf. With any board but "none" chosen,
# the seven numbers above are simply not what the C6 comes up on -- and nothing
# downstream says so, because the build succeeds and the image boots.
#
# "none" means "no preset; honour the explicit leaves". It is recorded here so
# check_c6_pin_config.py can compare it against the enabled
# CONFIG_ESP_HOST_DEV_BOARD_<X> symbol in sdkconfig.defaults and catch BOTH
# drifts: the selection vanishing, and the selection moving to another board.
C6_DEV_BOARD=none

# ---- RA8-side landing pins (EK-RA8D2 Pmod1 / J26) ----
# BENCH-PROVEN 2026-07-27: every hole below was scope-qualified end to end (a
# 2 Hz 3.3 Vpp square wave driven from the MCU, observed at the J26 hole), and
# the link then came up at SPI mode 3 / 1 MHz with zero bad checksums.
#
# DATA_READY (P402) took more than a scope trace to pin down. It transitions
# once per boot -- high while the C6 holds its queued boot event, low from the
# first completed transaction onward -- and then sits still, so a probe that
# counts transitions inside a transaction window scores it zero forever and
# reads its stillness as absence. It is identified here by that level history
# (it was the only side-band pin with it) plus the J26-8 hole's scope
# qualification. See examples/ek_ra8d2/hw_validated/c6/c6_spi_probe.
#
# "none" is the only accepted value for a signal that has no RA8-side landing
# pin, and it must pair with -1 on the C6 side above.
RA8_PIN_CS=P804
RA8_PIN_COPI=P801
RA8_PIN_CIPO=P802
RA8_PIN_SCK=P803
RA8_PIN_DATA_READY=P402
RA8_PIN_HANDSHAKE=P006
RA8_PIN_RESET=none

RA8_J26_CS=J26-1
RA8_J26_COPI=J26-2
RA8_J26_CIPO=J26-3
RA8_J26_SCK=J26-4
RA8_J26_DATA_READY=J26-8
RA8_J26_HANDSHAKE=J26-7
RA8_J26_RESET=none

# J26-5 and J26-11 are ground. J26-9 (P412) and J26-10 (P413) are unconnected;
# J26-9 is the hole reserved for the future host-driven EN / reset line that
# RA8_PIN_RESET=none records as absent today. There is no 3V3 wire -- the C6
# is self-powered over its own USB, which is why "the C6 is alive" is never
# evidence that the signal harness exists.

# ---- Required EK-RA8D2 SW4 DIP positions ----
# Four independent switches. Each owns a different thing, and conflating them
# is how the bank gets misread:
#
# SW4-1 / SW4-2 OFF+OFF selects the Pmod1 SPI position (UM Table 18 p 26).
# SW4-3 owns the OCTO-SPI MUX. ON is "Octo-SPI Inactive", which is what makes
#   the U6 / U9 bus switches connect P801..P804 to J26 AT ALL (UM Table 3
#   p 16). SW4-3 OFF leaves the on-board Octo-SPI flash in charge of those
#   pins, so J26-1..J26-4 are not electrically connected to the MCU -- the
#   board and the C6 both look perfectly healthy while nothing reaches the
#   wire.
# SW4-4 owns the ARDUINO / mikroBUS CONNECTORS -- not the Octo-SPI mux. OFF
#   deactivates them: a real trade-off, not an oversight, because the LSM6DSO
#   IMU Click is offline while the C6 link runs. SW4-3 ON + SW4-4 ON is not a
#   valid combination (#555).
#
# Getting this bank wrong is not a subtle failure mode: it was the entire
# 2026-07-26 outage, which was a MISREAD of these switches (SW4-4 ON, SW4-3
# OFF) and not a wiring fault. See #555 for why a wrong setting still reads as
# a plausible firmware red, and examples/ek_ra8d2/hw_validated/c6/c6_spi_probe
# for the preserved measurements taken while the nets were open at the mux.
RA8_SW4_1=OFF
RA8_SW4_2=OFF
RA8_SW4_3=ON
RA8_SW4_4=OFF

# ---- Flash defaults (proven over the CH343 UART bridge; see flash.sh) ----
# Addressed by DEVICE IDENTITY, not by ttyACM number. The bench carries three
# CDC devices (this bridge, the C6's native USB-JTAG port, and the board's
# J-Link OB VCOM) and a power cycle renumbers them: on 2026-07-27 this bridge
# moved from ttyACM1 to ttyACM0 and the board console took ttyACM1. Flashing
# the wrong device is not a subtle failure, but diagnosing it wastes an hour.
# Leave empty to let flash.sh resolve it (scripts/hil/lib/tty_resolve.sh); set
# it here or pass an explicit port to pin one when two bridges are attached.
C6_FLASH_PORT=
C6_FLASH_BAUD=460800
C6_FLASH_SIZE=16MB
C6_FLASH_MODE=dio
C6_FLASH_FREQ=80m

# ---- Bench USB cabling (the other half of the harness, recorded as data) ----
# The signal wires above are only half of what makes this bench work. The USB
# cabling was prose in flash.sh and the README, which is where a fact goes to be
# rediscovered: it is recorded here so ONE file answers "what is plugged into
# what", the same way it already answers "what is wired to what".
#
# C6_FLASH_BRIDGE / _USB_ID / _BY_ID_GLOB: the CH343 USB-UART bridge is the
# interface write_flash works over. flash.sh resolves it through
# scripts/hil/lib/tty_resolve.sh by the by-id glob rather than by ttyACM number
# -- see the C6_FLASH_PORT note above for why a number is not an address here.
C6_FLASH_BRIDGE=CH343
C6_FLASH_BRIDGE_USB_ID=1a86:55d3
C6_FLASH_BRIDGE_BY_ID_GLOB=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB_Single_Serial_*

# The C6's OWN native USB-JTAG port also enumerates as /dev/ttyACM<n> and looks
# like a perfectly good flashing target. It is not: write_flash over it fails
# with EPIPE partway through, leaving a half-written image. Never flash over it.
C6_FLASH_VIA_NATIVE_USB_JTAG=no

# The C6 is powered by its own USB cable, not by the RA8 board: there is no 3V3
# wire in the J26 harness (see the J26 note above). This is exactly why "the C6
# enumerates / the C6 is alive" is never evidence that the signal harness or the
# SW4 mux is correct -- the module powers up identically either way.
C6_POWER_SOURCE=own-usb
